@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@cindex overview
@cindex CGRA
@command{cgra} drives a Coarse-Grained Reconfigurable Array---a 4x4 mesh of
16-bit signed processing elements (PEs)---from the host over a serial link, on
top of the @code{libcgra} library.  The host reconfigures the array by loading
one 32-bit configuration word per PE, injects operands at the array edges,
advances the datapath a chosen number of cycles, and reads the register file
back.

The tool sits at the top of a small stack:

@example
   cgra  (this tool)     .cgra language, modes, pipelines, vector I/O
     |
   libcgra              transport, v2 protocol, kernels, sim: emulator
     |
   UART  or  sim:       real FPGA, or the in-process model
@end example

@cindex declarative configuration
Rather than hand-assembling configuration words, a user describes reusable
@dfn{modes} (array configurations) and @dfn{pipelines} (chains of modes) in a
declarative configuration language (@file{.cgra} files), and names them on the
command line.  The tool resolves named @dfn{device} profiles, compiles the
requested mode into per-PE words, streams the input vectors through it, and
formats the results.  Vectors move via standard input and output, so kernels
compose in ordinary UNIX pipelines.

@cindex emulator
@cindex sim:
@anchor{The emulator}
An in-process emulator is available as the pseudo-device @samp{sim:}, so every
command works without an FPGA; it re-implements the PE ALU and the protocol
byte for byte and is the golden reference the hardware is checked against.  It
can also be served on a pseudo-terminal (@command{cgra emulate}) so external
serial tools can talk to a virtual CGRA.

@cindex design intent
@section Why it looks the way it does
Each PE owns a single register, and external operands enter only at two edges
(north, per column; west, per row).  That one constraint shapes everything
above it: it is why element-wise kernels use a @dfn{diagonal} routing, why the
matrix--vector engine is @dfn{weight-stationary}, and why throughput on a real
link is bounded by serial round trips rather than by the number of PEs.
@xref{Dataflow}, for the details, and @ref{Protocol} for the wire format.
